No — don’t regas an R600a fridge/freezer yourself.

R600a is isobutane, a highly flammable gas. DIY “regas kits” are not appropriate for domestic fridges: correct repair requires finding and fixing the leak (if there is one), pressure-testing, replacing the filter-drier where needed, evacuating the system with a vacuum pump, and charging the exact manufacturer-specified amount by weight — often only a few dozen grams. A small overcharge or wrong procedure can stop it cooling properly or create a fire risk.

Given:
• compressor runs;
• back is warm;
• freezer fan runs;
• no frost at all;
• fridge and freezer are both warm,

a sealed-system problem such as loss of refrigerant, a restriction, or compressor failure is plausible. But it is not certain from those symptoms alone, and “adding gas” is not a diagnostic fix.

Before paying for a callout, the only useful low-risk test is a full defrost: unplug it, doors open for 24–48 hours, then restart it empty and allow 6–12 hours. If it still produces no cold/frost at all, it needs a refrigeration engineer or replacement.

When you call, ask for a fixed diagnostic/callout price and tell them: “R600a unit, compressor and freezer fan run, condenser/back gets warm, but neither compartment cools and there is no frost.” Ask whether they repair sealed-system leaks/compressor faults and whether the likely cost makes sense versus replacement. For an older or budget appliance, sealed-system repair often costs enough that replacement is more economical.
